Viva questions — short answers
• What is Git? A distributed version-control system that tracks source-code changes.
• What is GitHub? A hosted platform for Git repositories and collaboration.

Ubuntu DevOps & MLOps Practical Lab Manual Page 18
• What is a branch? An independent line of development.
• What is a merge conflict? A situation where Git cannot automatically combine competing edits.
• What is a pull request? A proposal to merge changes from one branch/fork into another repository or branch.
• What is CI? Automatically integrating and testing code changes.
• What is Jenkins? An automation server used to run build, test, and deployment pipelines.
• What is Docker? A platform for packaging and running applications in containers.
• What is Docker Compose? A tool to define and run multi-container applications.
• What is Kubernetes? A container orchestration platform.
• What is a Pod? The smallest deployable Kubernetes unit; it contains one or more containers.
• What is a Deployment? A Kubernetes resource that manages replica Pods and rolling updates.
• What is a Service? A stable network endpoint for accessing a set of Pods.
• What is HPA? Horizontal Pod Autoscaler; it adjusts replica count using metrics such as CPU utilization.
• Why monitor ML apps? To detect service failures, latency, resource pressure, and changes in prediction/data quality.
Troubleshooting quick notes
• `permission denied` with Docker: check whether your user is allowed to access the Docker daemon. Use the
institution's approved setup; logging out/in may be required after group changes.
• `ImagePullBackOff`: check image name/tag, registry login, and whether the cluster can pull the image.
• `CrashLoopBackOff`: inspect `kubectl logs POD_NAME` and `kubectl describe pod POD_NAME`.
• HPA shows ``: check metrics-server and `kubectl top pods`.
• Git push rejected: run `git pull --rebase origin main`, resolve any conflicts, then push.
• Jenkins cannot find a command: remember Jenkins runs as a service/agent user with a different PATH and
permissions.
Cleanup (only after your practical/demo)
# Stop Compose stack
docker compose down
# Remove Kubernetes resources created for this lab
kubectl delete -f k8s/hpa.yaml --ignore-not-found
kubectl delete -f k8s/service.yaml --ignore-not-found
kubectl delete -f k8s/deployment.yaml --ignore-not-found
# Stop Minikube (does not delete your project files)
minikube stop
NOTE: Do not run `docker system prune -a`, `docker compose down -v`, or delete the Minikube cluster unless you
understand that these actions can remove images, volumes, or cluster data.
